** ==============================================================================
** ABAQUS/STANDARD THERMO-METALLURGICAL MATERIAL CARD: 20MnCr5 (20MC5, 1.7147)
** Case-hardening steel, core composition 0.195 C - 0.25 Si - 1.25 Mn - 1.15 Cr (wt%)
** Units: mm, s, tonne, mJ, MPa, C
** Author: Victor Maia (vhfm08@gmail.com)
**
** ESTIMATED DATA. No measurement of this steel is behind this card. It is the
** 16MnCr5 card (Material_16MnCr5.inp) shifted with published empirical relations
** for the difference in composition, keeping its smooth property curves, its
** plateaus above the liquidus and its liquid-state strength floor.
**
** What changes with respect to 16MnCr5:
**  - Ac1 = 731 C, Ac3 = 831 C, Ms = 385 C: steel producer datasheet values for
**    20MnCr5 (Ovako Steel Navigator, calculated). The break points of the ferrite
**    and bainite property curves move with Ac1 / Ac3.
**  - Solidus 1470 C (16MnCr5: 1485 C): shift of -17 K from the usual linear
**    solidus formula for the composition difference; liquidus kept at 1530 C.
**  - Austenite -> ferrite: times of the 16MnCr5 card multiplied by the ratio
**    20MnCr5 / 16MnCr5 of the reaction-rate model of Li et al. (1998), factor
**    3.0 to 5.5 between 550 and 735 C.
**  - Austenite -> bainite: start / end times of the Li et al. (1998) model for
**    this composition (ASTM grain size 8), Bs = 545 C, JMA exponent n = 2.11.
**  - Flow curves: ferrite x 1.10, bainite x 1.09, martensite x 1.09, from the
**    hardness ratio of the Maynier et al. (1978) formulas; austenite unchanged.
**    The factor fades to 1.0 between 800 and 1100 C.
**  - Density, elastic constants, conductivity, expansion and latent heat: as
**    16MnCr5 (the two grades differ by 0.04 % C); consistent with the datasheet
**    values at ambient (40-45 W/m.K, 460-480 J/kg.K, 12E-6 1/K).
**  - Specific heat: the magnetic (Curie) peak, about 770 J/kg.K near 750 C, is
**    on the ferrite only; austenite is paramagnetic and has a smooth curve. The
**    two curves meet at Ac3 (650 J/kg.K at 831 C), so the ferrite -> austenite
**    switch does not cause a jump in heat capacity.
**  - Heating transformations: ferrite / bainite / martensite -> austenite end at
**    Ac3 = 831 C. Extra points above Ac3 follow a log-linear interpolation of the
**    times, because Abaqus interpolates the tables linearly.
**
** Not modelled: the enthalpy of the solid-state transformations (about 16 J/g
** for alpha -> gamma, against 280 J/g of latent heat of fusion). The framework
** has no input for it; a HETVAL subroutine would be needed.
**
** References:
**  - Li, Niebuhr, Meekisho, Atteridge, Metall. Mater. Trans. B 29 (1998) 661-672
**  - Andrews, J. Iron Steel Inst. 203 (1965) 721-727
**  - Maynier, Jungmann, Dollet, in Hardenability Concepts with Applications to
**    Steel, TMS-AIME (1978) 518-545
**  - Koistinen, Marburger, Acta Metall. 7 (1959) 59-60
**  - EN ISO 683-3 (chemical composition of 20MnCr5)
**
** The table type definitions are in Material_phase_trans_table_types.inp, which
** must be included once in the model.
** ==============================================================================
